  "text": "**Solution 1 (ratio 1) is wrong.** The inclusion Γ₁(𝔫℘^r) ⊂ Γ₁(𝔫℘) gives an injection of the level-𝔫℘ space into the level-𝔫℘^r space, but it does not force the multiplicities to agree. Level-raising produces new old-forms at the higher level (the ℘-stabilisations), so the multiplicity can increase. The claimed one-dimensionality of the α-eigenspace is asserted without justification.\n\n**Solution 3 (ratio q^{2D(r−1)}) is unsupported.** Its derivation is a guess: it counts a Borel-type quotient of the index, but then admits the diagonal characters collapse in characteristic p and says \"I'll go with\" the answer. The index [Γ₁(℘):Γ₁(℘^r)] being q^{3D(r−1)} does not translate directly into a multiplicity ratio, so nothing here establishes the formula.\n\n**Solution 2 (ratio r) is the most plausible.** By analogy with the classical picture, where the number of p-stabilisations grows with the level exponent, the multiplicity of a finite-slope eigensystem should grow linearly in r. I recall Hattori's work on Drinfeld cuspforms of higher level giving a linear-in-r multiplicity result, but I could not verify the exact statement or its normalisation here, so this rests on that recollection and the classical analogy rather than a checked derivation.\n\nCHOICE: 2",
